        "content": "<p>alexcrichton removed the review request for <a href=\"https://github.com/cfallin\">cfallin</a> from <a href=\"https://github.com/bytecodealliance/wasmtime/pull/15145\">PR #15145</a>.</p>",
        "id": 637627523,
        "sender_full_name": "Wasmtime GitHub notifications bot",
        "timestamp": 1793824523
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>alexcrichton requested <a href=\"https://github.com/alexcrichton\">alexcrichton</a> for a review on <a href=\"https://github.com/bytecodealliance/wasmtime/pull/15145\">PR #15145</a>.</p>",
        "id": 637627525,
        "sender_full_name": "Wasmtime GitHub notifications bot",
        "timestamp": 1793824523
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span aria-label=\"memo\" class=\"emoji emoji-1f4dd\" role=\"img\" title=\"memo\">:memo:</span> alexcrichton submitted <a href=\"https://github.com/bytecodealliance/wasmtime/pull/15145#pullrequestreview-5775590440\">PR review</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>This seems reasonable to me yeah, but I'm curious if we can remove one of the variants of <code>TransmitCheck</code> if it's not needed  for performance.</p>\n<p>Otherwise though I think it'd be good to have some docs somewhere explaining the situation a bit more as well. Maybe on <code>fn transmit_check</code> or maybe on <code>fn run</code>, I'm not sure which. The general idea though is that <code>T::typecheck</code> has already run at some point and the second-layer of checking here is basically \"is there more needed than that\".  I'm getting lost myself a bit in this, so I'm not entirely sure what such a comment would say though. For example lowering <code>StreamReader&lt;StreamAny&gt;</code> to a guest doesn't type-check further than <code>stream&lt;stream&lt;?&gt;&gt;</code> in the <code>typecheck</code> implementation up-front, but then sending a <code>StreamAny</code> on that stream does indeed type-check the <code>?</code>.</p>\n<p>I guess maybe I'm just a bit lost. Can you describe a bit more the situations where this is applying? For example what I can see is that if you create <code>StreamReader&lt;u8&gt;</code> then this is skipping a <code>typecheck</code> against <code>InterfaceType::U8</code> which, while fast in theory, still involves some lookups and dynamic dispatch. I don't fully understand the <code>Static</code> vs <code>Elided</code> distinction though...</p>\n</blockquote>",
        "id": 637632633,
        "sender_full_name": "Wasmtime GitHub notifications bot",
        "timestamp": 1793825928
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span aria-label=\"speech balloon\" class=\"emoji emoji-1f4ac\" role=\"img\" title=\"speech balloon\">:speech_balloon:</span> alexcrichton created <a href=\"https://github.com/bytecodealliance/wasmtime/pull/15145#discussion_r4473174060\">PR review comment</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>For the benchmarks that you're looking at, is this case needed? I feel like this PR would be simpler without this, but if it's needed for performance then it's needed</p>\n</blockquote>",
        "id": 637632634,
        "sender_full_name": "Wasmtime GitHub notifications bot",
        "timestamp": 1793825929
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span aria-label=\"speech balloon\" class=\"emoji emoji-1f4ac\" role=\"img\" title=\"speech balloon\">:speech_balloon:</span> alexcrichton created <a href=\"https://github.com/bytecodealliance/wasmtime/pull/15145#discussion_r4473186152\">PR review comment</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Personally I'd lean towards an auxiliary <code>bool</code> rather than a tag bit given the usage of this index for other purposes too</p>\n</blockquote>",
        "id": 637632635,
